  "response_text": "Round-robin scheduling can use a queue by organizing the threads (TCBs) in the order they should run. Each time a thread finishes its time slice or yields, it is removed from the front of the queue and placed at the back. The scheduler simply selects the thread at the front of the queue to run next. This is beneficial compared to an array because it naturally models the rotation of threads. A queue makes it easier to manage the order of execution dynamically without needing to track indices manually, and it simplifies adding or removing threads at runtime. It also avoids the need to search through the array to find the next thread to run.\\n",
